# CSS Styling: Fonts, Spacing, Images, and Hover Effects

## 1. Font Family

The `font-family` property is used to change the font style of text on a webpage. Different fonts can make a website look simple, modern, professional, or decorative.

### Syntax

```css
selector {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

### Example

```css
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

h1 {
    font-family: Georgia, serif;
}
```

In this example, the body text uses Arial, while the heading uses Georgia.

We can specify multiple fonts as fallbacks. If the first font is unavailable, the browser tries the next one.

```css
body {
    font-family: "Trebuchet MS", Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

### Common Font Families

| Font | Type | Use |
|---|---|---|
| Arial | Sans-serif | Simple and readable text |
| Georgia | Serif | Traditional and elegant headings |
| Verdana | Sans-serif | Clear text on screens |
| Times New Roman | Serif | Formal documents |
| Trebuchet MS | Sans-serif | Friendly, modern design |

